[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1221","stub":{"id":"1221","section":"05. Abiquiu Dam to confluence with Rio Grande","altname":"","river":"Rio Chama","difficulty":"ItoII","states":["USA-NME"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"migration-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"212","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"A single play wave accessed at the day use area below Abiquiu dam. The best put-in for running the lower stretch. Access is best from NM 96, which crosses the dam.","id":"107496","location":{"latitude":"36.239115","longitude":"-106.417282"},"name":"Abiquiu Wave","distance":"0","photoID":"892873","difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"Two-stage, low-head weir. River right is deepest, and river left forms a shelf that barely flows at 700 cfs.","id":"107497","location":{"latitude":"36.237116025969","longitude":"-106.41684519513"},"name":"Low-head dam","distance":"0.15","photoID":"0","difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"The only current cross-river fence in this stretch as of Spring 2020. It crosses just as the river hangs left around the bend, connecting to a large metal fence that is visible from up-stream. It is safely passable at around 700 cfs.","id":"107498","location":{"latitude":"36.235556565061","longitude":"-106.41574534715"},"name":"Low-hanging fence","distance":"0.3","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"portage","description":"A rock wier/ low-head dam diverts water to the acequia on river-left. Portage is available river right. May present hazard at low-flows.","id":"107500","location":{"latitude":"36.22943196328","longitude":"-106.39896691864"},"name":"Rock wier","distance":"1.35","photoID":"0","difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"portage","description":"The stretch above is mostly class I with several sections of swifter water and riffles. At 5.45 mi, the second rock wier/ low-head dam of the run crosses here, diverting water to river left. It was run-able in the center at 700 cfs for kayaks, but be aware entrapment hazards may exist at any flow. Multiple logs and rocks make up a natural feature that is likely change.","id":"107501","location":{"latitude":"36.212616394193","longitude":"-106.34964606016"},"name":"Rock wier","distance":"5.44","photoID":"0","difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"The previous section is easy going class I leading up to the take out on river right. First and easiest takeout near accesss to Highway 84","id":"107502","location":{"latitude":"36.204107747747","longitude":"-106.33152389105"},"name":"Optional Take-out, Hwy 84","distance":"6.9","photoID":"0","difficulty":"I","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"If you miss the first take out, or really need another half-mile of class I, the second take-out is river left just after passing under Highway 84. Another rock weir / low-head dam is present here and may be runnable with an eddy of river left for take out. As with the previous rock wiers, they are natural features and could present significant entrapment hazards at any level.\n\nThe river below is unlikely to be run. The next take out is at the crossing with 554, at 13.6 mi, where another rock weir diverts a significant flow to river right. Below is an addition 20 miles of water crossing multiple low-head damns, some impassable, until the confluence with the Rio Grande at approximately 33 river miles.","id":"107503","location":{"latitude":"36.210702431835","longitude":"-106.32638394155"},"name":"\"Last\" Take-out, Hwy 84","distance":"7.5","photoID":"0","difficulty":"I","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"100801038","title":null,"post_date":"2009-04-12 04:00:00+00","detail":"This run sucks. The best put in is immediately below the dam, right next to the signs that say \"NO ACTIVITY HERE\" in big letters. We did and nobody bothered us. There is a small rock dam about 500 yds downstream, followed by a low head dam/gaging station, GO RIGHT for the boaters chute. Just below this is a SOLID fence (read: large, square blocks with fencing both inside and between each other) that is strung across the river, portage right. The river wanders around for a 1/4 of a mile- go left in the split for some class II- and then it pours over a scary river-wide rock dam that diverts water off to a canal on river left. Below here there is maybe a quarter mile of spaced out class II+, another large split, and then that's it for the \"canyon\", the river then meanders through the open fields for the remainder of the run. There are a couple of small class II's in this stretch, a good amount of fast, moving water, and one smaller irrigation drop before you cross the road, takeout on the RIVER RIGHT side of the US 84 bridge. This is a fishermen-heavy stretch, so expect some hostility if you see any of them. All in all, save your energy and go run the Rio Ojo Caliente, the Rio Chama, or the Rio Grande, as this is one star at best!","user":null}],"photos":[{"id":"892865","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896630","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892865","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892865"}},{"id":"892873","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896640","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892873","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892873"}},{"id":"892872","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896639","description":null,"caption":"kayak","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892872","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892872"}},{"id":"892925","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896684","description":null,"caption":"Abiquiu Wave, 3.5 ft","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Ian L","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892925","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892925"}},{"id":"892866","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896631","description":null,"caption":"Rio Chama @05. Abiquiu Dam to confluence with Rio Grande","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892866","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892866"}},{"id":"892875","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896642","description":null,"caption":" on Rio Chama @05. Abiquiu Dam to confluence with Rio Grande","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892875","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892875"}},{"id":"892924","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896683","description":null,"caption":"Rio Chama @05. Abiquiu Dam to confluence with Rio Grande","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892924","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892924"}}],"detail":{"id":"1221","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"During the Fall/Winter of 2019/2020 the Army Corp. of Engineers and the New Mexico Fish and Wildlife office, completed a river enhancement of fish habitat/ fish ladder, by doing so they have created a play feature in that constriction. As we have tested flows the rapid is quickly becoming a great park and play feature. See the image gallery for pictures and flow rate. A big THANK YOU to NMFG and The Army Corp, along a huge THANK YOU to Ed Lucero who have the vision and the contacts to see this implemented.\n\n7.5 miles of class I/II meandering river is available below with several low-head dams. The take-out is just above or below the crossing with NM 84.\n\nFor information, see New Mexico Whitewater, by NM State Parks & Rivers of SW, by","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"7.5","correlations":[{"reachID":"1221","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"200","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"3000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"RIO CHAMA BELOW ABIQUIU DAM, NM","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"08287000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"212","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.47","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"36.2372222","longitude":"-106.4174167"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/08287000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/08287000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"migration-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"212","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["107496","107497","107498","107500","107501","107502","107503"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-106.42002950523832,36.239995201271675],[-106.4195848119225,36.23994681041506],